// DATAVERSE CHECK (v6.5): the tab behind the logo. Which tables and columns this page reads and writes really exist. Read only (v7.1): this page
// never makes a table or a column and has no prompt for it (contract ground rule 10); the Dataverse monitor builds what is missing, from the
// manifest this page saves in Hub config (MH MANIFEST).
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// With a Dataverse address (Xrm getClientUrl) it reads the TABLE DEFINITIONS (EntityDefinitions metadata), never rows: an empty table is
// judged by its definition, and types and sizes can be checked. A 404 counts as "missing" only when Dataverse itself says the table does
// not exist; when Dataverse leaves the column list out, the columns are "not checked", never "found" or "missing".
// Without an address (the page's own dataApi), or when the definitions get no answer at all, it asks the tables themselves through the
// Line order card's swProbe: one tiny read for the table, then one per column. That tells found from missing, but not types or sizes.
// The list (mhChkSpec) is rebuilt from the code: the column lists the page already has (DEPLOY_COLUMNS, EMP_TASK_FIELDS, TRAINING_FIELDS,
// WORKSHIFT_COLUMNS, MH_ACCESS_COLUMNS, BOARD_SELECT ...), what the Delegation dialog writes, LINE_PRIORITY_SPEC, plus craa5_remarks /
// craa5_remarkcategory (High fives), the craa5_employeenote table (Manager notes) and, since v6.6, the shift tables craa5_shiftboard and
// craa5_shiftarchive that Shift Start creates (craa5_taskassignment is legacy). Nothing here writes to Dataverse.
// Host rule: ES5 without iterables. Only arrays, plain objects, index loops, forEach / map and Promises are used.
// ============================================================================
